Most damage happens at the bottom of a cliff, where waves hit hardest. They cut a groove called a notch. The rock above hangs over it until it can no longer hold its own weight and crashes down. The cliff steps back, leaving a flat shelf of rock at its old foot.

Where waves attack a headland from both sides, a sequence follows. Cracks widen into caves. Caves on opposite sides meet and become an arch. The arch roof falls, leaving a stack. The stack is undercut and topples into a stump. The white chalk stacks of Old Harry Rocks, on England's south coast, show several of these stages side by side.

{{story:waves-how}}

Metamorphic means "changed in form". When rocks are buried deep, or caught where continents collide, heat and pressure change them. Their minerals rearrange into new crystals, while the rock stays solid, a bit like clay hardening in a [[kiln|kiln]] without melting.

Limestone becomes marble. The marble for the [[@taj-mahal|Taj Mahal]] came from Makrana in Rajasthan, and it began as sea-floor limestone. Shale becomes slate, which splits into flat sheets once used for school writing slates. Granite can become gneiss, a rock with wavy stripes.

{{story:taj-mahal}}

In the 1920s and 1930s, giant airships crossed oceans. In 1929, the Graf Zeppelin flew around the world. The Hindenburg was about 245 m long, more than three times the length of a jumbo jet. It had a dining room, cabins and a lounge with a piano.

The Empire State Building in [[@new-york|New York]] was even given a mooring mast so airships could tie up at the top and passengers could step off. Strong winds round the tower made that far too dangerous.

{{story:floating-ships}}

The purple frog of the Western Ghats lives underground, eating termites. It surfaces only during the monsoon to breed. It has a pointed snout, a tiny head and a puffy purple body, a bit like a deflated balloon with legs.

Local people knew it, but scientists only described it in 2003. Its closest relatives live in the Seychelles, islands in the Indian Ocean thousands of kilometres away. Long ago, India and the Seychelles were joined. When India [[@india-drift|drifted away]] across the ocean, the frogs' ancestors were split apart, and the two families have been [[evolve|evolving]] separately ever since.

{{story:india-drift}}

A photoelectric alarm has a little light and a sensor, set at an angle so the light doesn't normally hit the sensor. The chamber is dark inside, with a maze of walls to keep outside light out but let air in.

When smoke drifts in, the particles scatter light in all directions, like dust in a sunbeam. Some of it hits the sensor, and the alarm sounds. Photoelectric alarms are better at catching smouldering fires, such as a cigarette burning slowly into a sofa, which make bigger, thicker smoke.

{{story:fire-burns}}

Some of the world's earliest drainage systems were built in the [[@indus-cities|Indus Valley]], in what is now Pakistan and northwest India. In cities like Mohenjo-daro, Harappa and Dholavira, about 4,500 years ago, many houses had bathing areas connected to brick drains. These drains ran along the streets and were covered with bricks or stone slabs, with openings so workers could clean them.

Few ancient cities had anything so organised. [[@rome-city|Rome]] later built a great sewer called the Cloaca Maxima, but many cities around the world simply tipped waste into streets and rivers for thousands of years.

{{story:indus-cities}}

The word lakh comes from the Sanskrit *laksha*, and crore from *koti*. Both appear in old Indian texts.

Indian thinkers named large numbers very early. A passage in the Yajurveda, composed around 3,000 years ago, lists number words rising by tens from one to *parardha*, a million million. Later Buddhist and Jain texts went much further, naming numbers so vast they were used to describe the age of the universe. This comfort with big numbers grew alongside the [[@indian-numerals|Indian numerals]] and place value that the world now uses.

{{story:population}}

[[@friction|Friction]] is what makes the ball turn after bouncing. On a dry, dusty pitch, loose soil and cracks give the ball more to grip, so it can turn a long way. On a hard, green, grassy pitch, it skids on.

That is why spinners love Indian pitches, which often get drier and dustier as a five-day Test goes on. In the 1960s and 1970s, India's famous spin quartet, Bishan Singh Bedi, Erapalli Prasanna, B.S. Chandrasekhar and S. Venkataraghavan, won many matches this way. More recently, R. Ashwin took more than 500 Test wickets before retiring in 2024.

{{story:baseball-curve}}

India's first quiz stars were on [[@doordarshan-tech|Doordarshan]], when the country had just one TV channel. *Quiz Time*, hosted by Siddhartha Basu from the 1980s, pitted school and college teams against each other. The *Bournvita Quiz Contest*, which started on radio, later moved to TV with Derek O'Brien as quizmaster. Clever students became small celebrities, and schools held tryouts.

These shows were serious and clever. KBC kept the brainpower but added something new: money, music and drama.

{{story:memory}}

Many big cities sit on coasts or rivers. Rising seas and heavier rain make floods more common. Jakarta has a second problem: parts of it are sinking, because water is pumped out of the ground. Partly because of crowding and sinking, Indonesia has started building a new capital, Nusantara, on the island of Borneo.

China has a "sponge city" programme, started in 2015, that uses parks, ponds and special pavements to soak up rainwater instead of letting it flood the streets. Chennai and Bengaluru are restoring old lakes for the same reason.

{{story:earth-sinking-cities}}

Each time you open an app, there may be thousands of possible posts it could show you. The algorithm gives each one a score based on hundreds of clues. Did you watch a similar video to the end? Do you often reply to this friend? Is the post getting lots of comments quickly?

The highest-scoring posts go to the top. Posts that make people feel strongly, whether excited, angry or amused, often get more reactions, so they can spread faster. That is one reason rumours and arguments travel quickly online, and why it helps to learn [[@fake-news|how to tell if something is true]].

{{story:internet-citizens}}

Ashoka ordered messages called [[edict|edicts]] to be carved on rocks and polished stone pillars across his [[empire|empire]]. About thirty sites have been found, from Afghanistan to Karnataka.

Rock Edict XIII is the one about Kalinga. It is unusual: a king admitting that his victory caused grief. Strangely, this edict was left out in Kalinga itself. At Dhauli, near Bhubaneswar, the carvers placed special edicts instead, telling officials to treat the local people fairly. Perhaps it felt wrong to remind the Kalinga survivors of what had happened.

{{story:scripts-india}}

Almost every law follows the same pattern. If someone does this, then this happens. Law 196 says that if a man destroys the eye of another man of the same rank, his own eye shall be destroyed. This is where the famous phrase "an eye for an eye" comes from.

Other laws are about ordinary business. A doctor who heals a broken bone gets paid a set fee. Someone who leaves a canal gate open and floods a neighbour's field must pay for the lost crop. A builder whose house collapses and kills the owner is put to death.

{{story:why-rules}}

In 1582 Pope Gregory XIII introduced a new calendar, now called the Gregorian calendar. To catch up, 10 days were dropped: in Italy, Spain and other Catholic countries, Thursday 4 October 1582 was followed by Friday 15 October.

He also changed the rule for [[@leap-years|leap years]]. Years divisible by 100 would not be leap years unless they were also divisible by 400. So 1900 was not a leap year, but 2000 was. This keeps the calendar accurate to about one day in more than 3,000 years.

{{story:leap-years}}

Siraj ud-Daulah became Nawab in 1756, when he was in his early twenties. He was angry that the British were strengthening the walls of their fort in Calcutta without his permission and misusing their trading rights. In June 1756, his army captured the city.

Clive sailed up from Madras with soldiers and retook Calcutta in early 1757. He also attacked the French post at Chandernagore. Meanwhile, some of the most powerful people at Siraj's court were plotting against him. They included Mir Jafar, his army commander, and the Jagat Seths, a family of bankers so rich that they lent money to rulers.

{{story:kolkata}}

Green rice terraces climb Bali's hillsides like giant steps. They are watered by subak, a system of canals and tunnels that shares water from springs and rivers among farmers. It goes back to at least the 9th century.

Subak is run by farmers' groups together with water temples. Priests and farmers agree when each area will plant and flood its [[@rice|rice]] fields, so that everyone gets a fair share and pests have less chance to spread. UNESCO made it a World Heritage Site in 2012.

{{story:ramayana}}

Some Greek islands are volcanoes. Santorini is the most famous. Around 3,600 years ago, it blew apart in one of the largest eruptions of the last few thousand years. The middle of the island collapsed into a [[caldera|caldera]], a huge bowl that the sea filled.

Today white houses line the top of the cliffs, and the bay below is about 400 m deep in places. The volcano is not dead. Small islands in the middle of the bay were built by later eruptions, the most recent in 1950. Ash from the great eruption buried a [[@bronze-age|Bronze Age]] town called Akrotiri, preserving houses with painted walls, rather like [[@pompeii|Pompeii]].

{{story:greek-myths}}

France tried to build a canal first, starting in 1881. It was led by Ferdinand de Lesseps, who had built the Suez Canal. His plan was a sea-level canal with no locks. Landslides kept filling the cuttings, and yellow fever and malaria killed many thousands of workers. The French gave up in 1889.

The USA took over in 1904. Doctors had recently shown that mosquitoes spread these diseases. Teams drained swamps, covered water tanks and fumigated houses, and deaths fell sharply. Tens of thousands of workers came from the Caribbean, especially from Barbados, to do much of the hardest and most dangerous digging.

{{story:mosquito-diseases}}

India's saffron grows mainly around Pampore, a town near Srinagar in [[@kashmir|Kashmir]]. For most of the year the fields look like bare brown earth. Then, in late October, they turn purple almost overnight.

Whole families walk the rows at dawn with baskets, snapping off flowers. Back home, they sit together and pull out the three red threads from each one. The threads are dried, and they shrink to about a fifth of their fresh weight. In 2020 Kashmir saffron received a GI tag, a label that says it can only come from this region.

{{story:silk-road}}
